<!-- Saint Matthew (Levi)
Matthew (Ματθαῖος)/Levi bar Alphaeus: A Galilean Tax Collector
(Parallel to "Simon bar Jonah: A Galilean Fisherman")
Name, Identity, and Social Position
    • Called "Matthew" (Ματθαῖος, from Hebrew "Mattityahu," "gift of Yahweh") in Matthew 9:9 and in all four apostolic lists
    • Called "Levi" in the parallel calling accounts of Mark and Luke (Mark 2:14; Luke 5:27) — one man under two names, structurally the reverse problem of Bartholomew/Nathanael, where here the same Gospel writers who use "Levi" in the calling scene never use "Matthew" for him at all, while Matthew's own Gospel uses "Matthew" throughout
    • Identified as "the son of Alphaeus" (Mark 2:14) — worth flagging a further identity question here, since a different apostle, James, is also called "son of Alphaeus" (Mark 3:18); most interpreters treat these as two distinct men who happened to share a father's name, but this is a real ambiguity worth stating plainly rather than resolving too neatly
    • A tax collector (τελώνης) stationed at Capernaum, on the shore of the Sea of Galilee — placing him geographically within the same fishing community as Peter, Andrew, James, and John, despite his very different trade
    • Roman-employed tax collectors in Galilee were typically Jews contracted to collect tolls and duties for Herod Antipas or Rome directly, making them collaborators in the eyes of their own people
Family Structure & Responsibilities
    • Father named as Alphaeus (Mark 2:14) — the only family detail preserved in Scripture
    • Sufficient means to host "a great feast" for Jesus in his own house, attended by "a large company of tax collectors and others" (Luke 5:29) — implying real wealth and an existing social network among fellow tax collectors
The Tax Trade: Daily Reality
    • Worked at "the tax booth" in Capernaum (Matthew 9:9; Mark 2:14), likely collecting tolls on goods moving along trade routes near the Sea of Galilee, possibly under Herod Antipas's administration
    • Tax collectors were widely regarded as ritually and morally compromised due to routine extortion practices and collaboration with occupying powers
    • Financially literate and numerate by trade — a skill set that later tradition connects directly to his careful, structured Gospel writing
Religious Life
    • As a tax collector, ritually and socially marginalized within Jewish religious life — the Gospels consistently pair "tax collectors and sinners" as a single social category (Matthew 9:10-11; Luke 15:1)
    • No indication of prior religious devotion or messianic expectation recorded before his calling, unlike Nathanael or Andrew
Political Awareness
    • Occupied one of the most politically fraught positions available to a first-century Galilean Jew — directly employed within the machinery of Herodian/Roman taxation
    • Would have possessed firsthand, practical knowledge of Roman administrative and financial systems unmatched by any other apostle
Reputation in the Community
    • Despised by religious leaders and likely by much of the broader community — the Pharisees' immediate objection to Jesus eating with him assumes shared social contempt ("Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?", Matthew 9:11)
    • A figure defined before his calling almost entirely by social exclusion, standing in sharp contrast to Bartholomew's "Israelite indeed, in whom is no guile" reputation

Matthew the Called
(Parallel to "The Day the Nets Were Full" and the calling sections of Philip and Andrew)
    • Called directly by Jesus while sitting at the tax booth: "Follow me" (Matthew 9:9; Mark 2:14; Luke 5:27) — a direct, unmediated call like Philip's, rather than one brought through a family member or friend
    • Response is immediate and total: "he rose and followed him" (Matthew 9:9), leaving behind an established, lucrative position rather than a family fishing trade
    • Worth noting the scale of what was left behind: unlike Peter and Andrew's nets, Matthew's tax booth represented a secure, if socially costly, livelihood — his abandonment of it is arguably the more materially costly decision among the calling narratives so far
    • Immediately hosts a large feast in his own home for Jesus, gathering "tax collectors and sinners" to meet him (Matthew 9:10; Luke 5:29) — his first act as a disciple is itself an act of evangelism among his former social circle, paralleling Andrew's and Philip's instinct to bring others directly to Jesus
    • Provokes the Pharisees' objection and Jesus's response: "Those who are well have no need of a physician, but those who are sick... I came not to call the righteous, but sinners" (Matthew 9:12-13)
    • Included in the formal appointment of the Twelve, listed as "Matthew the tax collector" only in his own Gospel (Matthew 10:3) — a detail Mark and Luke's lists omit, which many readers take as a mark of personal humility in how he presents himself

Matthew's Journey
(Parallel to "Cephas's Journey": Walking on Water, Transfiguration, Denial/Restoration)
A Structurally Quiet Presence After the Calling
    • Like Philip, Bartholomew, and Thomas, Matthew has no further individually attributed action, question, or speech recorded anywhere in the Gospels after his calling narrative
    • This is a notable and now-recurring pattern worth stating directly: the called-and-feast episode is the entirety of Matthew's personal narrative footprint outside of his authorship of the Gospel itself
    • His major "presence" in the story is instead literary rather than narrative — the shaping hand behind an entire Gospel, discussed fully below
Presence Among the Twelve
    • Consistently listed among the Twelve in all four apostolic enumerations (Matthew 10:3; Mark 3:18; Luke 6:15; Acts 1:13)
    • Present, by inclusion in "the Twelve," for the full range of shared apostolic experiences — Transfiguration excluded, since only Peter, James, and John witnessed that directly — though no individual reaction of his own is recorded for any of them

Matthew the Apostle of the Early Church
(Parallel to Peter's leadership: Pentecost, Miracles, Imprisonment, Council)
Holy Spirit Praxis
    • Listed among the Twelve gathered in the upper room after the Ascension (Acts 1:13)
    • Present at Pentecost, though — continuing the pattern of Philip, Bartholomew, and Thomas — no individual action is attributed to him anywhere in Acts
Absence from Individual Narrative in Acts
    • Matthew recedes from the biblical narrative entirely after Acts 1:13, making this the fourth consecutive apostle in your series with this exact shape
    • His enduring contribution to the early church is textual rather than active — a distinction worth foregrounding, since it sets up his "Writings" section as unusually substantial compared to Philip, Bartholomew, and Thomas
The Council of Jerusalem
    • No individual role attributed to him in Acts 15
    • Presumed presence among "the apostles and elders" as a member of the Twelve, though not named specifically

Matthew's Later Ministry
(A distinctive tradition-dependent section, parallel to Thomas's and Bartholomew's "Later Ministry" sections)
    • Early Christian tradition is notably less unified on Matthew's later mission field than on Thomas's India or Bartholomew's Armenia
    • Various traditions (transmitted through Eusebius, Clement of Alexandria, and others) place his later ministry in Ethiopia, Persia, Parthia, or Macedonia — a wider spread of candidate locations than most other apostles, reflecting weaker consensus in the early sources
    • Some early traditions suggest he ministered first among Jewish communities in Judea before turning to a wider Gentile mission field, consistent with his Gospel's strong interest in demonstrating Jesus's fulfillment of Jewish messianic expectation
    • As with the other apostles' later ministries in this series, these accounts should be presented explicitly as tradition of varying and often conflicting reliability, rather than settled history

Matthew's Writings
(Parallel to "John's Writings" — his strongest point of contrast with Philip, Bartholomew, and Thomas)
Why Matthew Wrote
    • To present Jesus as the promised Jewish Messiah, the fulfillment of the Hebrew Scriptures and the true heir of David's throne
    • To address a primarily Jewish-Christian audience wrestling with Jesus's relationship to the Law and the Prophets
    • To organize Jesus's teaching into structured, memorable discourses suited for instruction and catechesis within the early church
    • To connect the story of Jesus explicitly and repeatedly to Old Testament prophecy, using the recurring formula "this was to fulfill what was spoken by the prophet"
The Gospel of Matthew
    • Traditionally regarded as the first Gospel written, and placed first in the New Testament canon, though the question of composition order remains debated among scholars (compare the "Synoptic Problem" and Marcan priority theory)
    • Structured around five major discourses (often seen as echoing the five books of the Torah): the Sermon on the Mount (5-7), the Missionary Discourse (10), the Parables of the Kingdom (13), instructions on church life (18), and the Olivet Discourse (24-25)
    • Opens with a genealogy tracing Jesus's lineage through David and Abraham (Matthew 1:1-17), establishing his Jewish messianic credentials from the first verse
    • Contains material unique among the Gospels, including the visit of the Magi (2:1-12), the flight to Egypt (2:13-15), and the parable of the sheep and the goats (25:31-46)
    • Concludes with the Great Commission: "Go therefore and make disciples of all nations... baptizing them... teaching them to observe all that I have commanded you" (Matthew 28:19-20)
    • Traditional authorship by the apostle Matthew is attested early (Papias, via Eusebius) but debated among modern scholars, some of whom see later editorial compilation drawing on Matthew's testimony rather than direct sole authorship — worth noting evenhandedly rather than asserting either position as settled
Major Themes
    • Jesus as the fulfillment of Jewish law and prophecy
    • The Kingdom of Heaven as a present and future reality
    • Righteousness that exceeds mere external observance of the Law
    • The church as the community entrusted with Christ's authority and mission
    • Discipleship as active obedience to Christ's teaching, not passive belief alone

The Final Journey
(Parallel to Peter's, Paul's, James's, John's, Philip's, Bartholomew's, and Thomas's Final Journeys)
What the Bible Tells Us
    • Scripture gives no account of Matthew's later life, ministry, or death — his last individual appearance in the biblical text is his listing among the Twelve in Acts 1:13, the same silence pattern seen with Philip, Bartholomew, and Thomas
Matthew's Ministry and Death (Early Christian Tradition)
    • Tradition is unusually divided on both the location and manner of his death, more so than most other apostles covered so far
    • Some traditions hold he died a natural death; others describe martyrdom, variously by sword, spear, or burning, in locations ranging from Ethiopia to Persia
    • This lack of consensus should be stated plainly rather than smoothed over — Matthew's page has the least historically stable martyrdom tradition in the series to this point
Matthew's Martyrdom
    • No single widely dominant account exists, unlike the comparatively consistent traditions for Thomas (spearing in India) or Bartholomew (flaying in Armenia)
    • Western tradition has tended to favor martyrdom, and later medieval art frequently depicts him being killed at an altar, but this reflects devotional tradition more than solid historical attestation

Matthew's Legacy
(Parallel to "Thomas's Legacy" / "Bartholomew's Legacy" / "Philip's Legacy")
    • Patron saint of accountants, bankers, tax collectors, and financial workers — a legacy directly and neatly shaped by his pre-calling profession, similar to how Bartholomew's martyrdom shaped his patronage of leatherworkers
    • Author of what tradition places as the first Gospel, giving him the most substantial literary legacy of any apostle covered in this series aside from John
    • Symbolically associated with the winged man (or angel) among the four evangelist symbols, traditionally linked to his Gospel's opening emphasis on Jesus's human genealogy
    • A model of dramatic vocational reversal: from a figure of social contempt and collaboration to an evangelist whose own calling narrative becomes a permanent statement about the scope of grace ("I came not to call the righteous, but sinners")
    • Represents, alongside Paul, one of the clearest "least likely candidate" transformation stories in the apostolic band — worth a direct cross-reference to Paul's page, since both move from complicity with an oppressive system (tax collection under Rome; persecution under the Sanhedrin) to central roles in the early church's theological foundation
    • Feast days observed separately in Eastern and Western traditions

